# LF everywhere, on every platform.
#
# Two separate failures came out of core.autocrlf=true, and they share a cause:
#
#   - A .sh committed from Windows reached macOS as CRLF and failed to parse, at
#     `case ... in`, with a message pointing eleven lines away from the cause.
#     It would also have silently broken the husky hooks, which have no extension
#     to key an attribute off.
#
#   - `git archive` applies the same conversion on export, so a committed
#     Podfile.lock arrived as CRLF. CocoaPods compares Podfile.lock against
#     Pods/Manifest.lock byte for byte, so the two differed on every line despite
#     being 2113 lines each, and the build died at "[CP] Check Pods Manifest.lock"
#     naming neither the lockfile nor the line endings.
#
# The second is the one that makes `* text=auto` worth having on its own: any
# tracked file something else compares byte-wise is corrupted by a checkout-time
# conversion, and there is no way to tell from the diff that it happened.
#
# `* text=auto` normalises on commit, so CRLF cannot enter the object store at
# all. `eol=lf` then keeps the working tree and `git archive` output LF on every
# platform, which is what the shell scripts and the byte comparison both need.
* text=auto eol=lf
